Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog audit** for `OrderProcessorTests.cs`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | 8 (class-wide) |
| **Total**| **9**       | **8 of 9 tests** |

Only **1 of 9** test methods is smell-free.

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH SEVERITY

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**Ref:** Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — tsDetect  
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)

```csharp
// ❌ if/else changes which assertion runs — one path is never verified
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Risk:** One branch is dead in every run; the test gives false confidence for the untested path.

**Fix — split into two parameterized tests:**
```csharp
[DataTestMethod]
[DataRow("SKU-1", 2, "StandardProcessed")]
[DataRow("SKU-PREMIUM", 5, "PremiumProcessed")]
public void ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus(string sku, int qty, string expected)
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem(sku, qty) } };
    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)
**Ref:** Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — tsDetect  
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
// ❌ No assertion — passes even if ProcessOrder returns garbage
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
```

**Risk:** 100% false confidence. The test contributes to coverage but verifies nothing.

**Fix — assert on the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };
    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(_db.WasOrderSaved(order.Id));
}
```

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test
**Ref:** Camara et al., SAST 2021 (correlated with flakiness)  
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)

```csharp
// ❌ Hard-coded 2-second sleep — flaky and slow
Thread.Sleep(2000);
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

**Risk:** Adds 2 s to every run; will intermittently fail under CI load. Camara et al. found Sleepy Tests are a strong predictor of flaky test suites.

**Fix — use a polling helper or make the async flow synchronous in tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };
    await processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);
    Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
}
```

